# SYNOPSIS

```swift
import Codecs

struct CodecManifest: Equatable, Sendable {
  var name: String
  var codecClass: String
  var types: [String]
  var extensions: [String]
  var magic: [Magic]
  var origin: String

  struct Magic: Equatable, Sendable {
    var offset: Int
    var bytes: [UInt8]
  }
  func recognizes(_ bytes: [UInt8]) -> Bool
}

struct CodecIndex: Sendable {
  var codecs: [CodecManifest]
  init(text: String, file: String) throws(CodecIndexError)
  var conflicts: [String] { get }
  func match(_ bytes: [UInt8], name: String? = nil) -> CodecManifest?
  static func generatedSource(_ manifests: [(path: String, text: String)])
    -> String
}

let builtInIndex: String
```

# DESCRIPTION

Each codec in `codecs/<class>/<name>/` has a `codec.manifest` that says what it decodes to and how to recognize its files:

```text
codec wav
class audio
type audio/wav
extension wav
magic 0 RIFF
magic 8 WAVE
```

A manifest starts with `codec <name>`, gives its `class` (`image` or `audio`), one or more `type` lines (MIME media types containing `/`), file name `extension` lines (without a leading dot), and `magic <offset> <bytes>` lines where `<bytes>` is literal text or `0x` followed by even-length hex digits.
Every `magic` line of a codec must match for `recognizes` to return true, and each codec must have a `class` and at least one `magic` or `extension` line.
`conflicts` reports any two codecs in an index that claim the same media type, the same extension, or identical magic rules.

`builtInIndex` holds the tree's `codec.manifest` files (`flac`, `wav`, `jpeg` and `png`) concatenated into one string.
`match` checks `bytes` (the start of a file) against every codec's magic rules first, returning the codec if one and only one matches; when none matches by content, it falls back to the extension of `name` after its last dot.
Codecs is tier 0, so a service links it as an app does; Tracker (tracker(4)) uses it with its own table of text and source extensions to set or infer `os:type` on files in `/data`.

